# Summary of the Bill (H.R. 1, "One Big Beautiful Bill Act" — Title VII, Subtitle A: Tax)

**Sec. 70001. References to the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, etc.**
This is a standard interpretive provision stating that all amendments and repeals in this title refer to the Internal Revenue Code of 1986. It also provides that Section 15 of the Code (which prevents rate changes from applying retroactively to transactions under binding contracts) does not apply to any tax rate changes made by this title.

**Sec. 70101. Extension and enhancement of reduced rates.**
This makes permanent the individual income tax rate brackets from the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act (10%, 12%, 22%, 24%, 32%, 35%, and 37%), which were previously scheduled to expire after 2025. The inflation adjustment is modified so that only brackets above 12% and above 22% are indexed for inflation. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70102. Extension and enhancement of increased standard deduction.**
This makes permanent the increased standard deduction and raises it further: to $23,625 for joint filers (up from $18,000) and $15,750 for single filers (up from $12,000), effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024. The inflation-indexing base year is updated from 2018 to 2025.

**Sec. 70103. Termination of deduction for personal exemptions other than temporary senior deduction.**
This permanently eliminates the personal exemption deduction (which had been suspended through 2025) but creates a new temporary "senior deduction" of $6,000 per qualified individual aged 65 or older, available for taxable years beginning before January 1, 2029. The deduction phases out by 6% of modified adjusted gross income above $75,000 ($150,000 for joint returns), requires a Social Security number, and applies only to joint filers who are married. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70104. Extension and enhancement of increased child tax credit.**
This makes permanent the expanded child tax credit and increases it from $2,000 to $2,200 per qualifying child, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024. It requires that both the taxpayer (or at least one spouse on a joint return) and the qualifying child have Social Security numbers issued by the Social Security Administration before the return due date. The maximum refundable portion remains $1,400, with both amounts indexed for inflation after 2024/2025 respectively.

**Sec. 70105. Extension and enhancement of deduction for qualified business income.**
This makes permanent the Section 199A qualified business income (QBI) deduction for pass-through businesses and raises the phase-in thresholds for the W-2 wage/property limitation from $50,000 to $75,000 for single filers and from $100,000 to $150,000 for joint filers. It creates a new minimum deduction of $400 (indexed after 2026) for taxpayers with at least $1,000 of aggregate QBI from active trades or businesses in which they materially participate. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70106. Extension and enhancement of increased estate and gift tax exemption amounts.**
This raises the unified estate and gift tax basic exclusion amount from $5 million (inflation-adjusted) to $15 million, indexed for inflation using 2025 as the base year. Effective for estates of decedents dying and gifts made after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70107. Extension of increased alternative minimum tax exemption amounts and modification of phaseout thresholds.**
This makes permanent the increased AMT exemption amounts ($109,400 for singles, $70,300 for married filing separately, and $1,000,000 for joint filers, all inflation-adjusted). The AMT exemption phaseout rate is increased from 25% to 50%, and the $1,000,000 joint-filer amount is indexed using 2025 as the base year. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70108. Extension and modification of limitation on deduction for qualified residence interest.**
This makes permanent the limitation on the mortgage interest deduction to $750,000 of acquisition indebtedness ($375,000 for married filing separately), which was scheduled to revert to $1 million after 2025. It also permanently allows mortgage insurance premiums to be treated as qualified residence interest. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70109. Extension and modification of limitation on casualty loss deduction.**
This makes permanent the rule limiting personal casualty loss deductions to federally declared disasters, which was scheduled to expire after 2025. It expands the rule to also allow deductions for "State declared disasters" — natural catastrophes, fires, floods, or explosions determined by a State's Governor (or DC's Mayor) together with the Secretary to be of sufficient severity. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70110. Termination of miscellaneous itemized deductions other than educator expenses.**
This makes permanent the suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions subject to the 2%-of-AGI floor, which was scheduled to expire after 2025. It creates a permanent exception allowing educator expenses (as defined in new Section 67(g)) as an itemized deduction, expanding the definition to include interscholastic sports administrators and coaches and nonathletic instructional supplies used as part of instructional activity. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70111. Limitation on tax benefit of itemized deductions.**
This replaces the old "Pease" limitation with a new rule reducing itemized deductions by 2/37 of the lesser of the deductions or taxable income above the 37% bracket threshold. The limitation does not apply for purposes of computing the Section 199A qualified business income deduction. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70112. Extension and modification of qualified transportation fringe benefits.**
This permanently eliminates the exclusion for qualified bicycle commuting reimbursements and the employer deduction for such benefits, which had been suspended through 2025. The monthly transit/parking benefit limits remain, with the inflation-indexing base year changed from 1998 to 1997. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70113. Extension and modification of limitation on deduction and exclusion for moving expenses.**
This makes permanent the suspension of the moving expense deduction and exclusion for employer-reimbursed moving expenses, which was scheduled to expire after 2025. It creates a permanent exception for members of the intelligence community (as defined in Section 3 of the National Security Act of 1947, excluding Armed Forces members) who move due to a change in assignment requiring relocation. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70114. Extension and modification of limitation on wagering losses.**
This makes permanent the gambling loss deduction limitation but changes it significantly: losses from wagering transactions are now deductible only up to 90% of such losses, and only to the extent of wagering gains for the year. The term "losses from wagering transactions" is expanded to include any other deductions incurred in carrying on wagering activity. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70115. Extension and enhancement of increased limitation on contributions to ABLE accounts.**
This makes permanent the increased ABLE account contribution limit, which allows designated beneficiaries to contribute up to the annual gift tax exclusion amount (indexed using 1996 as the base year) beyond the standard limit, for contributions made after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70116. Extension and enhancement of savers credit allowed for ABLE contributions.**
This makes permanent the Saver's Credit for ABLE account contributions and redefines "qualified retirement savings contributions" to include ABLE contributions, while repealing the SECURE 2.0 Act provision that had expanded the credit to include rollovers from retirement accounts to ABLE accounts. The credit's maximum applicable amount is increased from $2,000 to $2,100, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2026.

**Sec. 70117. Extension of rollovers from qualified tuition programs to ABLE accounts permitted.**
This makes permanent the ability to roll over funds from a Section 529 qualified tuition program (529 plan) to an ABLE account, which was scheduled to expire after 2025. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70118. Extension of treatment of certain individuals performing services in the Sinai Peninsula and enhancement to include additional areas.**
This makes permanent the treatment of the Sinai Peninsula of Egypt as a "qualified hazardous duty area" for purposes of the combat-zone tax benefits for members of the Armed Forces. It adds Kenya, Mali, Burkina Faso, and Chad as qualified hazardous duty areas, applicable while service members receive hostile fire/imminent danger special pay under 37 U.S.C. § 310. Effective January 1, 2026.

**Sec. 70119. Extension and modification of exclusion from gross income of student loans discharged on account of death or disability.**
This makes permanent the exclusion from gross income for student loan discharges due to death or total and permanent disability, which was scheduled to expire after 2025. The exclusion covers both student loans (including those under the Higher Education Act of 1965) and private education loans under the Consumer Credit Protection Act, and now requires the taxpayer to include their Social Security number on the return. Effective for discharges after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70120. Limitation on individual deductions for certain state and local taxes, etc.**
This raises the state and local tax (SALT) deduction cap from $10,000 to $40,000 for 2025 ($40,400 for 2026, then increasing 1% annually through 2029, reverting to $10,000 in 2030), with half these amounts for married filing separately. For taxpayers with modified AGI above $500,000 ($505,000 in 2026, indexed 1% annually), the cap is reduced by 30% of the excess income but never below $10,000. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70201. No tax on tips.**
This creates a new above-the-line deduction (Section 224) of up to $25,000 per year for "qualified tips" — cash tips received in occupations that customarily received tips on or before December 31, 2024, as listed by the Treasury Secretary within 90 days of enactment. The deduction phases out by $100 for each $1,000 of modified AGI above $150,000 ($300,000 joint), requires a Social Security number, excludes tips from businesses that are "specified service trades" (like law or health), and is available to non-itemizers. It also extends the Section 45B tip credit to beauty services (barbering, hair care, nail care, esthetics, body/spa treatments), adds tip-reporting requirements to Forms W-2 and information returns, and the deduction terminates after December 31, 2028.

**Sec. 70202. No tax on overtime.**
This creates a new above-the-line deduction (Section 225) of up to $12,500 ($25,000 for joint returns) per year for "qualified overtime compensation" — the premium portion of overtime pay required under Section 7 of the Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938 (i.e., the "half" premium above the regular rate). The deduction phases out by $100 for each $1,000 of modified AGI above $150,000 ($300,000 joint), requires a Social Security number, is available to non-itemizers, and must be reported on Form W-2. The deduction terminates after December 31, 2028.

**Sec. 70203. No tax on car loan interest.**
This creates a deduction (Section 163(h)(4)) of up to $10,000 per year for interest on loans incurred after December 31, 2024, to purchase "applicable passenger vehicles" for personal use — cars, minivans, vans, SUVs, pickup trucks, or motorcycles under 14,000 lbs whose final assembly occurred in the United States. The deduction phases out by $200 for each $1,000 of modified AGI above $100,000 ($200,000 joint), requires the vehicle identification number on the return, and excludes fleet sales, commercial vehicles, leases, salvage-title vehicles, and scrap/parts vehicles. Lenders must report loan interest of $600 or more annually on new Section 6050AA returns, and the provision applies to taxable years 2025 through 2028.

**Sec. 70204. Trump accounts and contribution pilot program.**
This creates "Trump accounts" (Section 530A) — tax-advantaged IRAs for children under 18 that function like traditional IRAs, with annual contributions capped at $5,000 (indexed after 2027), invested only in low-fee (≤0.1%) index funds tracking the S&P 500 or similar U.S. equity indices, with no distributions before age 18. Employers may contribute up to $2,500 annually (indexed after 2027) tax-free to employees' or dependents' Trump accounts under new Section 128, and tax-exempt organizations may make "qualified general contributions" to classes of beneficiaries. A new pilot program (Section 6434) provides a $1,000 government-funded contribution to Trump accounts of U.S. citizen children born between January 1, 2025, and December 31, 2028, with $410 million appropriated to Treasury through September 30, 2034, and penalties of $500 (negligence) or $1,000 (fraud) for improper claims.

**Sec. 70301. Full expensing for certain business property.**
This makes permanent 100% bonus depreciation under Section 168(k) for qualified property acquired after January 19, 2025, removing the phase-down schedule that had reduced the allowance to 40% in 2025. It also makes permanent 100% expensing for certain plants (fruit-bearing trees, vines, etc.) planted or grafted after January 19, 2025, and shortens the required production period rules for longer-production property. Taxpayers may elect a reduced 40% (or 60% for longer-production property) allowance for the first taxable year ending after January 19, 2025.

**Sec. 70302. Full expensing of domestic research and experimental expenditures.**
This restores immediate deduction of domestic research and experimental expenditures under new Section 174A, reversing the 2017 TCJA requirement to amortize them over 5 years; taxpayers may alternatively elect 60-month amortization. Foreign research expenditures remain subject to mandatory 15-year amortization under revised Section 174. Small businesses (meeting the Section 448(c) gross receipts test) may elect retroactive application to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2021, and all taxpayers may elect to deduct remaining unamortized domestic R&E from 2022–2024 either immediately or ratably over two years.

**Sec. 70303. Modification of limitation on business interest.**
This permanently restores the EBITDA standard (adding back depreciation, amortization, and depletion) for computing adjusted taxable income under the Section 163(j) business interest deduction limitation, by removing the sunset of the 2021–2023 rule. It also expands the "floor plan financing interest" exception to include trailers and campers designed for temporary living quarters (recreational, camping, or seasonal use) that are towed by or affixed to motor vehicles. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70304. Extension and enhancement of paid family and medical leave credit.**
This makes permanent the Section 45S employer credit for paid family and medical leave and expands it to allow employers to claim the credit based on insurance premiums paid for paid family/medical leave policies (in lieu of wages), with the credit rate determined without regard to whether leave was actually taken. The minimum employment duration for qualifying employees is reduced from 1 year to 6 months at the employer's election, and a new requirement that employees customarily work at least 20 hours per week is added. The aggregation rule is tightened (excluding separate lines of business and wage rates as legitimate reasons for differing policies), and state-mandated or state-paid leave counts toward the policy but not the credit. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70305. Exceptions from limitations on deduction for business meals.**
This creates exceptions to the 50% limitation (and full disallowance) on business meal deductions for meals provided on fishing vessels, fish processing vessels, or fish tender vessels (as defined in 46 U.S.C. § 2101), and at fish processing facilities located in the U.S. north of 50 degrees north latitude and outside a metropolitan statistical area. Effective for amounts paid or incurred after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70306. Increased dollar limitations for expensing of certain depreciable business assets.**
This raises the Section 179 immediate expensing limit for depreciable business assets from $1,000,000 to $2,500,000, and the phase-out threshold from $2,500,000 to $4,000,000. The inflation-indexing base year for these amounts is updated from 2017 to 2024. Effective for property placed in service in taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70307. Special depreciation allowance for qualified production property.**
This creates a new 100% depreciation allowance (Section 168(n)) for "qualified production property" — the portion of new nonresidential real property used as an integral part of manufacturing, production, or refining of tangible personal property, placed in service in the U.S. before January 1, 2031, with construction beginning after January 19, 2025 and before January 1, 2029. Office space, administrative services, lodging, parking, sales, research, software development, and engineering activities are excluded, and food/beverage production in the same building as retail sale is disqualified. A 10-year recapture rule applies if the property ceases qualifying use, and the Secretary may extend the placed-in-service deadline for acts of God.

**Sec. 70308. Enhancement of advanced manufacturing investment credit.**
This increases the Section 48D advanced manufacturing investment credit rate (for semiconductor manufacturing facilities and property) from 25% to 35%. Effective for property placed in service after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70309. Spaceports are treated like airports under exempt facility bond rules.**
This adds spaceports to the types of qualified exempt facility bonds under Section 142(a)(1), allowing tax-exempt private activity bonds to finance spaceport facilities — defined as facilities at or near launch/reentry sites used for spacecraft manufacturing, assembly, repair, flight control, launch/reentry services, or crew/cargo transfer. Spaceports on land leased by a governmental unit from the United States qualify, no general public use requirement applies, and federal rent or user fee payments do not disqualify the bonds under the federally guaranteed bond prohibition. Effective for bonds issued after enactment.

**Sec. 70311. Modifications related to foreign tax credit limitation.**
This adds a new rule (Section 904(b)(5)) for allocating deductions to foreign-source net CFC tested income (GILTI) for foreign tax credit limitation purposes: the Section 250 deduction and taxes on such income are allocated to it, while interest expense and R&E expenditures are not (unless directly allocable), increasing the foreign tax credit limitation basket. Conforming amendments update cross-references in Sections 904(d) and 951A(f). Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70312. Modifications to determination of deemed paid credit for taxes properly attributable to tested income.**
This increases the deemed-paid foreign tax credit for GILTI inclusions from 80% to 90% of foreign taxes (Sections 960(d)(1) and 78), reducing the effective tax cost of GILTI. However, it disallows the foreign tax credit for 10% of foreign taxes with respect to previously taxed net CFC tested income distributions excluded under Section 959(a). The increase applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025; the disallowance applies to taxes with respect to amounts excluded after June 28, 2025.

**Sec. 70313. Sourcing certain income from the sale of inventory produced in the United States.**
This adds a new rule (Section 904(b)(6)) providing that income from the sale outside the U.S. of inventory produced in the U.S. and sold through a foreign office or fixed place of business is treated as 50% foreign-source (rather than 100% foreign-source under the Section 863(b) title-passage rule) for foreign tax credit limitation purposes. This increases the U.S.-source portion, which can increase the foreign tax credit limitation. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70321. Modification of deduction for foreign-derived deduction eligible income and net CFC tested income.**
This reduces the Section 250 deduction rates: the foreign-derived intangible income (FDII) deduction drops from 37.5% to 33.34%, and the GILTI deduction drops from 50% to 40%. Combined with the 90% deemed-paid credit in Sec. 70312, this sets the effective GILTI rate at roughly 12.6–14%. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70322. Determination of deduction eligible income.**
This expands the foreign-derived deduction eligible income (FDDEI) definition to include income and gain from sales or dispositions of intangible property and depreciable/amortizable/depletable property (previously excluded), effective for dispositions after June 16, 2025. It also limits expense apportionment against FDDEI to only expenses properly allocable to such income (excluding interest and R&E), effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025 — both changes increase the FDII deduction.

**Sec. 70323. Rules related to deemed intangible income.**
This renames "global intangible low-taxed income" (GILTI) to "net CFC tested income" (NCTI) throughout the Code and repeals the Section 951A(b) "deemed return" deduction (10% of qualified business asset investment) and the 20% QBAI-related exclusion, simplifying the GILTI computation. It also renames "foreign-derived intangible income" to "foreign-derived deduction eligible income" (FDDEI). Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70331. Extension and modification of base erosion minimum tax amount.**
This makes the Base Erosion and Anti-abuse Tax (BEAT) permanent and raises its rate from 10% to 10.5%, while repealing the scheduled increase to 12.5% that would have applied after 2025. Conforming amendments update cross-references, including a correction changing "6038B" to "6038A" in Section 59A(h)(2)(B). Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70341. Coordination of business interest limitation with interest capitalization provisions.**
This adds new Section 163(j)(10) providing that the business interest limitation applies to interest regardless of whether it would otherwise be deducted or capitalized (e.g., under Sections 263A(f) or 263(g)), with the allowed amount applied first to capitalized interest, then to deductible interest. Interest capitalized under Sections 263(g) or 263A(f) is excluded from the definition of "business interest," and carried-forward disallowed interest is not treated as subject to capitalization provisions. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70342. Definition of adjusted taxable income for business interest limitation.**
This modifies the Section 163(j)(8) definition of adjusted taxable income (ATI) to add back amounts included in gross income under Sections 951(a) (Subpart F), 951A(a) (GILTI/NCTI), and 78 (gross-up), along with the related Section 245A and 250 deductions attributable to those inclusions. This increases ATI for U.S. shareholders of foreign corporations, loosening the interest deduction limitation. Effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70351. Permanent extension of look-thru rule for related controlled foreign corporations.**
This makes permanent the Section 954(c)(6) look-through rule, which excludes dividends, interest, rents, and royalties received by one controlled foreign corporation (CFC) from a related CFC from Subpart F income, which was scheduled to expire after 2025. Effective for taxable years of foreign corporations beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70352. Repeal of election for 1-month deferral in determination of taxable year of specified foreign corporations.**
This repeals the Section 898(c)(2) election allowing specified foreign corporations (large foreign corporations with U.S. shareholders) to defer their taxable year change by one month, requiring them to conform to the "first required year" rule. A transition rule applies the change to the first taxable year beginning after November 30, 2025, treating it as initiated by the corporation with the Secretary's consent, with regulations for allocating foreign taxes between years.

**Sec. 70353. Restoration of limitation on downward attribution of stock ownership in applying constructive ownership rules.**
This restores the "downward attribution" rule (new Section 958(b)(4)), providing that Section 318(a)(3) attribution from a non-U.S. person to a U.S. person does not apply — meaning foreign-owned U.S. entities are not attributed stock owned by their foreign owners. It creates new Section 951B, which applies Subpart F and GILTI rules to "foreign controlled United States shareholders" (U.S. persons owning more than 50% of a "foreign controlled foreign corporation" — a foreign corporation majority-owned by foreign persons with U.S. shareholders), closing a loophole where foreign persons could avoid CFC rules by interposing foreign corporations. Effective for taxable years of foreign corporations beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70354. Modifications to pro rata share rules.**
This rewrites Section 951(a) to change how a U.S. shareholder's pro rata share of a CFC's Subpart F income is determined: it is now based on stock owned on any day during the CFC year (rather than the last day), with inclusion in the shareholder's taxable year containing the last day of stock ownership. Section 956 (investment in U.S. property) inclusions are determined based on ownership on the last day the corporation is a CFC. A transition rule provides that certain dividends paid by CFCs on or before June 28, 2025 (or after that date but before the CFC's first taxable year beginning after December 31, 2025) are not treated as dividends for purposes of the old rules if they don't increase U.S. taxable income. [end of text]


